\section{Introduction}

When a Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct agent attempts to boil water in ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, it must track temperature thresholds, container states, and heat source interactions across dozens of steps. Internal LLM reasoning hallucinates these state transitions---predicting water has boiled when the stove was never activated, or failing to model that metal conducts heat differently than glass---triggering agent death with a $-100$ penalty. Deterministic external computation (environment simulators, code interpreters) eliminates such hallucinations by executing exact state transitions. The natural question: when should an agent route computation externally versus reasoning internally?

Existing routing approaches \cite{toolformer-2024,art-2023,chameleon-2024} frame tool selection as a capability or efficiency decision---matching subtasks to tools that can solve them. This framing ignores a potentially dominant signal: routing to \emph{avoid catastrophic failure}. Understanding what routing actually learns requires counterfactual analysis, but the standard per-step oracle approach degenerates under sparse rewards. On ScienceWorld, where non-zero reward arrives only at episode termination, 98.2\% of steps produce identical scores regardless of modality---no step-level signal exists to train a router (\S\ref{sec:collection}). Keyword-based heuristics (\S\ref{sec:rule}) also fail structurally: ScienceWorld actions use generic verbs (\texttt{pour}, \texttt{move}, \texttt{activate}) rather than science-specific terminology, providing no lexical basis for routing decisions.

We propose \emph{episode-level counterfactual analysis}: run complete episodes under pure-internal (ReAct~\cite{react-2023}) and pure-deterministic strategies, then compare episode outcomes to generate routing labels (\S\ref{sec:collection}). Across 11 ScienceWorld task types (245 episodes), the full deterministic advantage is $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$ points. Excluding episodes where either strategy causes agent death, this gap collapses to $\Delta_{\text{no\_death}} = +2.84$---a Death Avoidance Factor (DAF; \S\ref{sec:daf}) of 90.41\%. The per-task correlation between death rate differential and performance gap is $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$), confirming that death avoidance is the primary mechanism across task types (\S\ref{sec:death-decomp}). Death rates differ sharply: 42.9\% for internal reasoning versus 16.3\% for deterministic computation. Episode-level labels reflect this asymmetry: of 245 episodes, 86 prefer deterministic, 9 prefer internal, and 150 show no preference---96.2\% of oracle routing decisions match an unconditional always-deterministic strategy (\S\ref{sec:baselines}).

Our contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Episode-level counterfactual methodology with death avoidance decomposition.} We introduce a framework that generates routing labels from episode-level outcome comparisons and decomposes the routing signal via the DAF metric and death-aware labeling (\S\ref{sec:collection}, \S\ref{sec:daf}). This overcomes per-step oracle degeneracy (98.2\% tie rate) in sparse-reward environments.
\item \textbf{Death avoidance dominates routing signal on ScienceWorld.} Across 11 task types (245 episodes), death avoidance accounts for 90.41\% of the routing signal between Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct internal reasoning and deterministic computation. Non-death differences are small ($\Delta_{\text{no\_death}} = +2.84$, 9.6\% of $\Delta_{\text{full}}$) but reveal residual routing value---e.g., \texttt{find-non-living-thing} has zero deaths yet $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +9.58$ (\S\ref{sec:death-decomp}). Router training as a diagnostic probe shows counterfactual labels encode learnable patterns at the representation level (logit accuracy 74.5\%, balanced accuracy 79\%) despite a generation-level expression gap (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Method}

\subsection{Counterfactual Episode Collection}
\label{sec:collection}

Per-step oracle labeling assigns routing labels by comparing single-step outcomes under each computation modality. Under sparse terminal rewards, this approach degenerates: on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, 98.2\% of steps produce identical intermediate scores regardless of modality, yielding no routing signal. We instead perform episode-level counterfactual analysis.

For each task instance, we execute two complete episodes under identical initial conditions (same random seed, same task variation). The \emph{pure-internal} episode follows the ReAct paradigm \cite{react-2023}, where the LLM interleaves chain-of-thought reasoning with environment actions. The \emph{pure-deterministic} episode uses the environment simulator to compute exact state transitions for each action. In both cases the LLM selects which action to take; deterministic computation replaces only the state-transition prediction, not action selection---equivalent to giving the agent access to a perfect world model.

Each episode pair produces a tuple $(s^{\text{int}}, s^{\text{det}}, d^{\text{int}}, d^{\text{det}})$, where $s$ denotes the terminal score and $d \in \{0,1\}$ indicates agent death. Episodes are capped at 30 steps. The LLM backbone is Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct served via vLLM. We evaluate on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, which spans 30 task types covering physical and chemical reasoning with a $-100$ penalty upon agent death. After prescreening (5 episodes per type for signal estimation), we perform targeted full collection on 11 task types, yielding 245 episode pairs (20--40 per type).

\subsection{Death-Aware Labeling}
\label{sec:labeling}

Each counterfactual pair receives an episode-level routing label based on $(s^{\text{int}}, s^{\text{det}}, d^{\text{int}}, d^{\text{det}})$. ScienceWorld scores are integer-valued, so we use exact (in)equality with no threshold. The label is assigned by one of four mutually exclusive cases:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Death asymmetry}: $d^{\text{int}} \neq d^{\text{det}}$ $\rightarrow$ the surviving strategy is preferred.
    \item \textbf{Both dead}: $d^{\text{int}} = d^{\text{det}} = 1$ $\rightarrow$ \texttt{no\_preference}.
    \item \textbf{Score comparison}: both survive and $s^{\text{det}} \neq s^{\text{int}}$ $\rightarrow$ the higher-scoring strategy is preferred.
    \item \textbf{Tied alive}: both survive and $s^{\text{det}} = s^{\text{int}}$ $\rightarrow$ \texttt{no\_preference}.
\end{enumerate}

Across 245 episodes, this yields 86 (35.1\%) \texttt{det\_preferred}, 9 (3.7\%) \texttt{internal\_preferred}, and 150 (61.2\%) \texttt{no\_preference}. The near-absence of \texttt{internal\_preferred} labels combined with the dominance of \texttt{no\_preference} indicates that the deterministic strategy's advantage is concentrated in death avoidance rather than superior task performance when both agents survive.

\subsection{Death Avoidance Factor}
\label{sec:daf}

To quantify the contribution of death avoidance to routing signal, we define the Death Avoidance Factor (DAF):
\begin{equation}
    \text{DAF} = 1 - \frac{\Delta_{\text{no\_death}}}{\Delta_{\text{full}}}
\end{equation}
where $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ is the mean score gap ($s^{\text{det}} - s^{\text{int}}$) across all episodes and $\Delta_{\text{no\_death}}$ is the same gap restricted to episodes where neither strategy results in death. DAF $= 1$ indicates all routing signal stems from death avoidance; DAF $= 0$ indicates all signal stems from non-death performance differences. When $\Delta_{\text{full}} = 0$, DAF is undefined, as no routing signal exists to decompose; we report DAF only for task types where $|\Delta_{\text{full}}| > 0$.

On 245 episodes across 11 task types: $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$, $\Delta_{\text{no\_death}} = +2.84$, yielding DAF $= 90.41\%$. The cross-task consistency is strong: the Pearson correlation between per-task death rate differential (internal minus deterministic) and $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ is $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$) across 11 task types, indicating that knowing how much more frequently the internal strategy dies is nearly sufficient to predict the full performance gap.

\subsection{Rule-Based Baseline}
\label{sec:rule}

As a simple routing heuristic, we test keyword-based rules that route to deterministic computation when the action contains science-related verbs. We evaluate three keyword lists of decreasing breadth:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{v1} (20 generic verbs including \emph{connect}, \emph{move}): matches 85.8\% of actions, degenerating to always-deterministic.
    \item \textbf{v2} (17 science-specific verbs: \emph{heat}, \emph{boil}, \emph{melt}, \emph{freeze}, \ldots): matches 4.9\%, degenerating to always-internal.
    \item \textbf{v3} (12 state-change verbs): matches 2.8\%, degenerating to always-internal.
\end{itemize}

The structural cause is that ScienceWorld actions use generic interaction verbs (\texttt{pick up}, \texttt{move}, \texttt{connect}) rather than science-specific terms. No keyword granularity bridges the gap between near-total and negligible match rates, precluding meaningful routing discrimination. This structural failure motivates observation-based approaches that use environment observations and agent state as routing features rather than action keywords.

\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:exp-setup}

We evaluate on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, a text-based interactive environment for elementary science tasks with sparse terminal rewards and a $-100$ penalty upon agent death. Using the counterfactual methodology described in \S\ref{sec:collection}, we collect 245 episode pairs across 11 task types (20--40 episodes per type). The LLM backbone is Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, served via vLLM, with episodes capped at 30 steps. The internal-reasoning strategy follows the ReAct paradigm \cite{react-2023}; the deterministic strategy uses the environment simulator for exact state transitions.

\subsection{Death Avoidance Decomposition}
\label{sec:death-decomp}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_death_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Per-task death rate differential (internal $-$ deterministic) vs.\ full performance gap ($\Delta_{\text{full}}$). Each point is one task type ($n=11$). Pearson $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$).}
\label{fig:decomposition}
\end{figure}

The full performance gap between deterministic and internal strategies across 245 episodes is $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$ points. When we exclude all episodes where either strategy results in agent death, the gap collapses to $\Delta_{\text{no\_death}} = +2.84$. The resulting Death Avoidance Factor (\S\ref{sec:daf}) is 90.41\%: approximately 90\% of the deterministic strategy's advantage over internal reasoning is attributable to death avoidance rather than superior task performance when both agents survive.

This concentration is consistent across task types. The Pearson correlation between per-task death rate differential (internal minus deterministic) and $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ is $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$), as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:decomposition}. Knowing how much more frequently the internal strategy dies is nearly sufficient to predict the full performance gap for a given task type. The internal strategy dies in 42.9\% of episodes compared to 16.3\% for deterministic computation---a 2.6$\times$ ratio that drives the bulk of the observed score difference.

The episode-level label distribution reflects this asymmetry. Of 245 episodes, 86 are labeled \texttt{det\_preferred}, only 9 \texttt{internal\_preferred}, and 150 \texttt{no\_preference}. The near-absence of internal-preferred labels---combined with the dominance of no-preference labels---indicates that when internal reasoning does not lead to catastrophic failure, it performs comparably to deterministic computation. The few internal-preferred episodes arise from tasks where deterministic computation's rigid action space occasionally produces suboptimal trajectories despite avoiding death.

One task reveals the residual non-death signal. \texttt{find-non-living-thing} ($n=40$) is the only task type with zero deaths under both strategies, yet it shows $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +9.58$, entirely from non-death performance differences. This confirms that routing value beyond death avoidance exists: deterministic computation can provide advantages through more accurate state tracking even when agents survive. However, this signal is not the dominant factor across the benchmark. Among the 10 death-prone task types, death avoidance overwhelms non-death differences. For example, \texttt{measure-melting-point-unknown} ($n=20$) exhibits death rates of 55\% (internal) and 25\% (deterministic), producing $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +30.05$---a gap driven primarily by the 30-percentage-point death rate differential rather than non-death performance.

When both modalities survive, their task performance is near-equivalent ($\Delta_{\text{no\_death}} = +2.84$). The value of deterministic computation in this environment is concentrated in preventing catastrophic failures, not in providing positive task guidance.

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Strategy & Score & 95\% CI & Death \% \\
\midrule
Always-Internal & $-39.07$ & [$-44.89$, $-33.37$] & 42.9 \\
Always-Deterministic & $-9.47$ & [$-13.66$, $-5.11$] & 16.3 \\
Random & $-22.38$ & [$-27.75$, $-17.09$] & 27.8 \\
Oracle & $-6.58$ & [$-10.65$, $-2.43$] & 14.3 \\
\midrule
Trained Router & $-10.70$ & [$-15.25$, $-6.38$] & 17.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Episode-weighted mean scores on ScienceWorld (11 task types, 245 episodes). Confidence intervals computed via bootstrap. The oracle selects the higher-scoring strategy per episode; 96.2\% of its decisions match the always-deterministic strategy.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} reports episode-weighted mean scores and death rates for four unconditional strategies and the trained router. Always-deterministic ($-9.47$) outperforms always-internal ($-39.07$) by 29.60 points, consistent with $\Delta_{\text{full}}$. The oracle, which selects the higher-scoring strategy per episode, improves only modestly to $-6.58$, with 96.2\% of its routing decisions matching always-deterministic. This \emph{oracle degeneracy} indicates that the optimal episode-level routing policy closely resembles unconditional deterministic computation---the routing signal is insufficient to improve upon simply always choosing the deterministic strategy. Random routing ($-22.38$) falls between the two pure strategies, with a death rate (27.8\%) proportional to its 50/50 allocation.

The trained router (Qwen3-1.7B implicit router, seed 42) scores $-10.70$, 1.23 points below always-deterministic. Its death rate (17.6\%) is comparable to always-deterministic (16.3\%), confirming that the router avoids degeneration but does not improve upon unconditional deterministic routing in end-to-end deployment.

Keyword-based routing (\S\ref{sec:rule}) structurally fails to provide meaningful discrimination. ScienceWorld actions use generic interaction verbs (\texttt{pick up}, \texttt{move}, \texttt{connect}) rather than science-specific terms, causing keyword match rates to cluster at extremes: near-total (v1: 85.8\%, degenerating to always-deterministic) or negligible (v2: 4.9\%, v3: 2.8\%, both degenerating to always-internal). No keyword granularity bridges this gap.

\subsection{Router Training as Diagnostic}
\label{sec:router-diagnostic}

A natural question is whether the episode-level counterfactual labels can train a step-level router. We investigate this as a diagnostic probe of label informativeness rather than a routing system proposal, testing two architectures.

\paragraph{Classification head.} We append a linear classification head to the LLM's hidden representations, trained on episode-level routing labels. Across all configurations tested---class weighting, focal loss, minority undersampling, and temperature scaling---the classifier degenerates to predicting always-deterministic (0\% internal recall in all cases). The episode-level labeling granularity does not transfer to step-level classification through this architecture.

\paragraph{Implicit router.} We fine-tune a smaller model (Qwen3-1.7B) to internalize routing decisions within its generation process. At the logit level, routing patterns emerge: accuracy reaches 74.5\% with balanced accuracy of 79\%, demonstrating that the counterfactual labels encode learnable routing structure beyond what a degenerate always-deterministic predictor captures. At the generation level, however, accuracy drops to 65.36\% with internal recall of 51.5\%, revealing a gap between logit-level pattern recognition and generation-level expression.

End-to-end deployment (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}) confirms this gap: the trained router scores $-10.70$ (CI [$-15.25$, $-6.38$]), 1.23 points below always-deterministic ($-9.47$). Its 97.0\% simulator invocation ratio means it routes to internal reasoning only 3\% of the time---near-always-deterministic behavior despite non-degenerate logit-level routing patterns.

Across four random seeds (42, 123, 456, 789), balanced accuracy is stable at $58.1 \pm 0.8\%$---only 8 percentage points above random chance. However, internal recall varies from 51.5\% to 81.8\% (30+ percentage point range), indicating that the decision boundary orientation is seed-dependent while overall discriminability is not. This pattern---consistent weak signal with unstable thresholds---further supports the interpretation that the labels encode minimal routing structure beyond death avoidance: there is enough signal for the model to avoid degeneration, but not enough for robust, seed-invariant routing decisions.

These results yield two observations. First, the counterfactual labels encode genuine routing patterns at the representation level---the logit-level success rules out uninformative labels as the cause of classification head failure, pointing instead to the episode-to-step granularity mismatch as the bottleneck. Second, while the labels reveal learnable routing patterns at the logit level, generation-level expression of these patterns remains an open challenge. Whether this gap stems from the labeling granularity, the generation decoding process, or model capacity is under investigation; step-level counterfactual labeling may be a productive direction for bridging it.

\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Tool-Use Learning.}
A line of work teaches LLMs when and how to invoke external tools.
Toolformer \cite{toolformer-2024} inserts API calls via self-supervised filtering;
ART \cite{art-2023} delegates tool selection to LLM reasoning chains;
Chameleon \cite{chameleon-2024} composes diverse modules under LLM-as-planner orchestration;
and ReTool \cite{retool-2026} applies reinforcement learning to learn implicit code-interpreter invocation for mathematical reasoning.
These methods learn \emph{which tool} to use or \emph{whether} to use one, but do not analyze what signal drives the learned routing policy.
We differ in three ways: (1)~we generate labels via \emph{episode-level counterfactual analysis}---running complete trajectories under competing strategies---rather than self-supervised or RL signals; (2)~we frame routing as catastrophic-failure prevention rather than general tool selection; and (3)~by training an explicit lightweight router decoupled from the backbone, we can diagnose \emph{what} the routing signal encodes, revealing that ${\sim}90\%$ is death avoidance.

\paragraph{Adaptive Computation.}
Several recent methods adapt reasoning effort per step.
CogRouter \cite{cogrouter-2026} achieves state-of-the-art on ScienceWorld (82.3\%) by routing among cognitive depth levels via Cooperative Policy Optimization.
ARES \cite{ares-2026} trains a lightweight router to select per-step reasoning effort, reducing token cost by 52.7\%.
Mixture-of-Recursions \cite{mixture-of-recursions-2025} applies adaptive depth at the architecture level via per-token recursive routing.
These methods route \emph{how much} to reason; we route \emph{which modality} to use (internal LLM reasoning vs.\ deterministic computation).
More importantly, none quantify the composition of their routing signal.
Our counterfactual decomposition reveals that death avoidance accounts for ${\sim}90\%$ of the performance gap---the contribution is this empirical finding and the diagnostic methodology, not the router architecture itself.

\paragraph{World Model Routing.}
ITP \cite{itp-2026} augments agents with LLM-based world model lookahead, adaptively adjusting the simulation horizon based on task progress.
Dyna-Think \cite{dynathink-2025} integrates world model simulation into the reasoning loop, matching 685B-parameter performance with a 32B model at half the token cost.
Both route to \emph{approximate} world models that inherit LLM hallucination failures.
Our deterministic computation modality provides \emph{exact} state transitions, and episode-level counterfactual analysis reveals that this exactness matters almost exclusively for death avoidance: non-death performance is near-equivalent across modalities ($\Delta_\text{nd}$ accounts for only 9.6\% of the full gap).

\paragraph{Agent Frameworks.}
ReAct \cite{react-2023} interleaves reasoning and acting in a single LLM call and serves as a foundational framework for interactive agents, including our internal-reasoning baseline.
ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022} provides the evaluation environment: 30 science task types requiring multi-step reasoning over rule-governed state transitions with catastrophic failure penalties.
MTRouter \cite{mtrouter-2026} routes between different LLMs on ScienceWorld for cost optimization, but targets model-level selection rather than reasoning-modality selection.
Our work complements these efforts by asking a different question: not \emph{how} to build a better agent framework, but \emph{what} a learned router actually captures.
The answer---that routing signal is dominated by catastrophic-failure prevention---suggests that future agent designs should prioritize safety-aware computation allocation over general-purpose reasoning enhancement.

\section{Conclusion}

We presented an episode-level counterfactual analysis methodology for diagnosing routing behavior in modular LLM agent systems. Applied to 245 episodes across 11 ScienceWorld task types, our analysis reveals that death avoidance accounts for 90.41\% of the routing signal between internal LLM reasoning and deterministic computation ($\Delta_\text{full}$ = +29.60 vs.\ $\Delta_\text{nd}$ = +2.84). This concentration is corroborated by a near-perfect correlation between per-task death rate differentials and routing benefit ($r$ = 0.9698, $p$ = 8.12$\times$10$^{-7}$), the oracle router's 96.2\% agreement with always-deterministic, and a trained router's near-random balanced accuracy of 58.1\%.

\section*{Limitations}

Our analysis is conducted on a single benchmark (ScienceWorld) with a single LLM backbone (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct), and generalization to other interactive environments and model scales remains to be established. ScienceWorld's severe $-100$ death penalty may amplify the death avoidance signal; environments with less catastrophic failure penalties could exhibit a lower DAF, though the qualitative finding---death avoidance as a major component of routing signal---may still hold. Our counterfactual labels operate at episode granularity; step-level labeling with denser reward signals is a natural extension that could improve both decomposition resolution and router training. The gap between logit-level and generation-level routing accuracy in the implicit router warrants further investigation.

