\begin{abstract}
Modular LLM agents that route between neural reasoning and deterministic computation promise improved reliability in rule-governed environments, yet understanding \emph{what} drives effective routing remains unclear. Per-step oracle labeling degenerates in sparse-reward settings---in ScienceWorld, 98\% of steps yield tied outcomes, rendering step-level analysis uninformative. We propose episode-level counterfactual analysis: running complete episodes under pure strategies and comparing final outcomes to derive per-episode routing labels. Applying this methodology to 245 episodes across 11 task types with a Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct backbone, we find that death avoidance accounts for 90.41\% of the routing signal (Death Avoidance Factor). Per-task death rate differentials correlate near-perfectly with routing benefit ($r$ = 0.9698, $p$ = 8.12$\times$10$^{-7}$). The oracle router agrees with always-deterministic on 96.2\% of decisions, and a trained router achieves only 58.1\% balanced accuracy---near random. These results indicate that deterministic computation's value is concentrated in catastrophic failure prevention rather than positive task guidance.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

When a Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct agent attempts to boil water in ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, it must track temperature thresholds, container states, and heat source interactions across dozens of steps. Internal LLM reasoning hallucinates these state transitions---triggering agent death with a $-100$ penalty. Deterministic external computation (environment simulators, code interpreters) eliminates such hallucinations by executing exact state transitions. The natural question: when should an agent route computation externally versus reasoning internally?

Existing routing approaches \cite{toolformer-2024,art-2023,chameleon-2024} frame tool selection as a capability decision---matching subtasks to tools that can solve them. This framing ignores a potentially dominant signal: routing to \emph{avoid catastrophic failure}. The standard per-step oracle approach degenerates under sparse rewards: on ScienceWorld, 98.2\% of steps produce identical scores regardless of modality (\S\ref{sec:collection}). Keyword-based heuristics (\S\ref{sec:rule}) also fail: ScienceWorld actions use generic verbs (\texttt{pour}, \texttt{move}) rather than science-specific terminology.

We propose \emph{episode-level counterfactual analysis}: run complete episodes under pure-internal (ReAct~\cite{react-2023}) and pure-deterministic strategies, then compare episode outcomes to generate routing labels (\S\ref{sec:collection}). Across 245 episodes, the full deterministic advantage is $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$ points; excluding death episodes, it collapses to $\Delta_{\text{no\_death}} = +2.84$---a Death Avoidance Factor (DAF; \S\ref{sec:daf}) of 90.41\%. The per-task correlation between death rate differential and performance gap is $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$). Of 245 episodes, 96.2\% of oracle routing decisions match always-deterministic (\S\ref{sec:baselines}).

Our contributions: \textbf{(1)} We introduce episode-level counterfactual methodology with the DAF metric, overcoming per-step oracle degeneracy in sparse-reward environments (\S\ref{sec:collection}, \S\ref{sec:daf}). \textbf{(2)} We show death avoidance accounts for 90.41\% of routing signal on ScienceWorld, with router training revealing learnable patterns at the representation level despite a generation-level expression gap (\S\ref{sec:death-decomp}, \S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}).

\section{Method}

\subsection{Counterfactual Episode Collection}
\label{sec:collection}

Per-step oracle labeling degenerates under sparse terminal rewards: on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, 98.2\% of steps produce identical intermediate scores regardless of modality. We instead perform episode-level counterfactual analysis.

