\subsection{Counterfactual Episode Collection}
\label{sec:collection}

Per-step oracle labeling assigns routing labels by comparing single-step outcomes under each computation modality. Under sparse terminal rewards, this approach degenerates: on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, 98.2\% of steps produce identical intermediate scores regardless of modality, yielding no routing signal. We instead perform episode-level counterfactual analysis.

For each task instance, we execute two complete episodes under identical initial conditions (same random seed, same task variation). The \emph{pure-internal} episode follows the ReAct paradigm \cite{react-2023}, where the LLM interleaves chain-of-thought reasoning with environment actions. The \emph{pure-deterministic} episode uses the environment simulator for exact state transitions. In both cases the LLM selects actions; deterministic computation replaces only state-transition prediction---equivalent to a perfect world model.

Each episode pair produces $(s^{\text{int}}, s^{\text{det}}, d^{\text{int}}, d^{\text{det}})$, where $s$ denotes terminal score and $d \in \{0,1\}$ indicates agent death. Episodes are capped at 30 steps. The LLM backbone is Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct served via vLLM. We evaluate on ScienceWorld, which spans 30 task types covering physical and chemical reasoning with a $-100$ penalty upon agent death. After prescreening, we perform full collection on 11 task types, yielding 245 episode pairs (20--40 per type).

\subsection{Death-Aware Labeling}
\label{sec:labeling}

Each counterfactual pair receives an episode-level routing label based on $(s^{\text{int}}, s^{\text{det}}, d^{\text{int}}, d^{\text{det}})$. The label is assigned by one of four mutually exclusive cases:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Death asymmetry}: $d^{\text{int}} \neq d^{\text{det}}$ $\rightarrow$ the surviving strategy is preferred.
    \item \textbf{Both dead}: $d^{\text{int}} = d^{\text{det}} = 1$ $\rightarrow$ \texttt{no\_preference}.
    \item \textbf{Score comparison}: both survive and $s^{\text{det}} \neq s^{\text{int}}$ $\rightarrow$ the higher-scoring strategy is preferred.
    \item \textbf{Tied alive}: both survive and $s^{\text{det}} = s^{\text{int}}$ $\rightarrow$ \texttt{no\_preference}.
\end{enumerate}

Across 245 episodes, this yields 86 (35.1\%) \texttt{det\_preferred}, 9 (3.7\%) \texttt{internal\_preferred}, and 150 (61.2\%) \texttt{no\_preference}---indicating the deterministic advantage is concentrated in death avoidance.

\subsection{Death Avoidance Factor}
\label{sec:daf}

To quantify the contribution of death avoidance to routing signal, we define:
\begin{equation}
    \text{DAF} = 1 - \frac{\Delta_{\text{no\_death}}}{\Delta_{\text{full}}}
\end{equation}
where $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ is the mean score gap ($s^{\text{det}} - s^{\text{int}}$) across all episodes and $\Delta_{\text{no\_death}}$ restricts to episodes where neither strategy dies. DAF $= 1$ indicates all routing signal stems from death avoidance; DAF $= 0$ indicates all signal comes from non-death performance differences.

On 245 episodes: $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$, $\Delta_{\text{no\_death}} = +2.84$, yielding DAF $= 90.41\%$. The Pearson correlation between per-task death rate differential and $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ is $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$) across 11 task types.

\subsection{Rule-Based Baseline}
\label{sec:rule}

As a simple routing heuristic, we test keyword-based rules that route to deterministic computation when actions contain science-related verbs. ScienceWorld actions use generic interaction verbs (\texttt{pick up}, \texttt{move}, \texttt{connect}) rather than science-specific terms, causing keyword match rates to cluster at extremes: near-total (v1: 85.8\%, degenerating to always-deterministic) or negligible (v2: 4.9\%, v3: 2.8\%, degenerating to always-internal). No keyword granularity provides meaningful routing discrimination.

\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:exp-setup}

We evaluate on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, a text-based interactive environment for elementary science tasks with sparse terminal rewards and a $-100$ penalty upon agent death. Using the counterfactual methodology described in \S\ref{sec:collection}, we collect 245 episode pairs across 11 task types. The LLM backbone is Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct served via vLLM, with episodes capped at 30 steps.

\subsection{Death Avoidance Decomposition}
\label{sec:death-decomp}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_death_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Per-task death rate differential (internal $-$ deterministic) vs.\ full performance gap ($\Delta_{\text{full}}$). Each point is one task type ($n=11$). Pearson $r = 0.9698$ ($p = 8.12 \times 10^{-7}$).}
\label{fig:decomposition}
\end{figure}

The full performance gap between deterministic and internal strategies is $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$ points. When we exclude all episodes where either strategy results in death, the gap collapses to $\Delta_{\text{no\_death}} = +2.84$ (DAF $= 90.41\%$). This concentration is consistent across task types (Figure~\ref{fig:decomposition}). The internal strategy dies in 42.9\% of episodes vs.\ 16.3\% for deterministic---a 2.6$\times$ ratio driving the bulk of the score difference.

Of 245 episodes, 86 are \texttt{det\_preferred}, only 9 \texttt{internal\_preferred}, and 150 \texttt{no\_preference}. When internal reasoning does not lead to catastrophic failure, it performs comparably to deterministic computation.

One task reveals residual non-death signal. \texttt{find-non-living-thing} ($n=40$) is the only task type with zero deaths under both strategies, yet shows $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +9.58$, entirely from non-death performance differences. Among death-prone task types, death avoidance overwhelms non-death differences: \texttt{measure-melting-point-unknown} ($n=20$) exhibits death rates of 55\% (internal) vs.\ 25\% (deterministic), producing $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +30.05$.

