Table~\ref{tab:baselines} reports episode-weighted mean scores and death rates. Always-deterministic ($-9.47$) outperforms always-internal ($-39.07$) by 29.60 points, consistent with $\Delta_{\text{full}}$. The oracle improves only modestly to $-6.58$, with 96.2\% of its routing decisions matching always-deterministic. This \emph{oracle degeneracy} indicates the optimal episode-level routing policy closely resembles unconditional deterministic computation. Random routing ($-22.38$) falls between the two pure strategies.

The trained router (Qwen3-1.7B implicit router, seed 42) scores $-10.70$, 1.23 points below always-deterministic, with a comparable death rate (17.6\% vs.\ 16.3\%). It avoids degeneration but does not improve upon unconditional deterministic routing.

\subsection{Router Training as Diagnostic}
\label{sec:router-diagnostic}

We investigate whether episode-level labels can train a step-level router, as a diagnostic probe of label informativeness rather than a routing system proposal.

\paragraph{Classification head.} A linear classification head appended to the LLM's hidden representations degenerates to predicting always-deterministic (0\% internal recall) across all configurations tested---class weighting, focal loss, minority undersampling, and temperature scaling.

\paragraph{Implicit router.} Fine-tuning Qwen3-1.7B yields logit-level accuracy of 74.5\% with balanced accuracy of 79\%, demonstrating learnable routing structure beyond degenerate prediction. At the generation level, accuracy drops to 65.36\% (internal recall 51.5\%), revealing a representation-to-generation gap. End-to-end deployment confirms this: the router invokes the simulator 97.0\% of the time---near-always-deterministic despite non-degenerate logit-level patterns.

Across four random seeds (42, 123, 456, 789), balanced accuracy is stable at $58.1 \pm 0.8\%$---only 8 percentage points above random. Internal recall varies from 51.5\% to 81.8\%, indicating seed-dependent decision boundaries with consistent weak discriminability. This pattern supports that the labels encode minimal routing structure beyond death avoidance.

These results yield two observations. First, counterfactual labels encode genuine routing patterns at the representation level---ruling out uninformative labels and pointing to the episode-to-step granularity mismatch as the bottleneck. Second, generation-level expression of these patterns remains an open challenge; step-level counterfactual labeling may be a productive direction.

\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Tool-Use Learning.}
Toolformer \cite{toolformer-2024} inserts API calls via self-supervised filtering; ART \cite{art-2023} delegates tool selection to reasoning chains; Chameleon \cite{chameleon-2024} composes modules under LLM-as-planner orchestration; ReTool \cite{retool-2026} uses RL for implicit code-interpreter invocation. These methods learn \emph{which tool} to use but do not analyze what signal drives routing. We generate labels via episode-level counterfactual analysis, revealing ${\sim}90\%$ is death avoidance.

\paragraph{Adaptive Computation.}
CogRouter \cite{cogrouter-2026} achieves 82.3\% on ScienceWorld via cognitive depth routing; ARES \cite{ares-2026} reduces token cost by 52.7\% with per-step effort selection; Mixture-of-Recursions \cite{mixture-of-recursions-2025} applies adaptive depth via per-token recursive routing. These route \emph{how much} to reason; we route \emph{which modality}. Our decomposition reveals death avoidance accounts for ${\sim}90\%$ of the performance gap.

\paragraph{World Model Routing.}
ITP \cite{itp-2026} augments agents with world model lookahead; Dyna-Think \cite{dynathink-2025} integrates world model simulation into reasoning, matching 685B performance with 32B at half the token cost. Both route to \emph{approximate} world models; our deterministic modality provides \emph{exact} transitions, and counterfactual analysis reveals this exactness matters almost exclusively for death avoidance ($\Delta_\text{nd}$ is only 9.6\% of the full gap).

\paragraph{Agent Frameworks.}
ReAct \cite{react-2023} provides our internal-reasoning baseline; ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022} is the evaluation environment. MTRouter \cite{mtrouter-2026} routes between LLMs for cost optimization but targets model-level rather than modality-level selection. Our work asks \emph{what} a router captures: the answer is dominated by catastrophic-failure prevention, suggesting future designs should prioritize safety-aware computation allocation.

\section{Conclusion}

We presented episode-level counterfactual analysis for diagnosing routing behavior in modular LLM agents. Across 245 ScienceWorld episodes, death avoidance accounts for 90.41\% of routing signal ($\Delta_\text{full}$ = +29.60 vs.\ $\Delta_\text{nd}$ = +2.84). This concentration is corroborated by near-perfect cross-task correlation ($r$ = 0.9698), oracle degeneracy (96.2\% agreement with always-deterministic), and near-random trained router accuracy (58.1\%). Future work should extend to step-level counterfactual labeling and evaluate generalization to environments with different failure modes.

\section*{Limitations}

Our analysis uses a single benchmark (ScienceWorld) with a single LLM backbone (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct), and generalization to other interactive environments and model scales remains to be established. The $-100$ death penalty may amplify DAF; environments with less catastrophic penalties could exhibit lower DAF, though the qualitative finding may still hold. Step-level labeling with denser rewards is a natural extension. The logit-to-generation gap in the implicit router warrants further investigation.

